"Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to generously give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?","Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?",7,7,7,7,"Let me solve this step by step.

1. First, let's calculate how many total glue sticks Mr. Jackson needs:
   * Number of students = 27
   * Glue sticks per student = 2
   * Total glue sticks needed = 27 × 2 = 54 glue sticks

2. Now, we need to determine how many packs to buy:
   * Each pack contains 8 glue sticks
   * Number of packs = 54 ÷ 8 = 6.75 packs

3. Since Mr. Jackson can only buy whole packs:
   * He must round up to 7 packs to have enough glue sticks
   * 7 packs × 8 glue sticks = 56 glue sticks total
   * This will give him 2 extra glue sticks

Therefore, Mr. Jackson needs to buy 7 packs of glue sticks.

